extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Column"
	- pagedescription = "Column charts in Tablechart: one bar per row, standing on the baseline, with the number above or inside it."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Column
				p.lead One bar per row, standing on the baseline.

	+section("Column chart")
		p Each row of the table is a column. The number is shown above it.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption
						b Apples picked
						br
						span thousands
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							tr
								th Summer
								td 72
							tr
								th Autumn
								td 70
							tr
								th Winter
								td 91

			+codeblock('HTML')
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption
						b Apples picked
						br
						span thousands
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							+n
							// …one row per column

	+section("Numbers inside the columns")
		p Add #[code data-chart-labels="inside"] to show each number inside its column, at the top. To align it at the bottom of the column or in the middle instead, see #[a(href=url('/styling/text.html')) Text]. If a column is too short for its number, the number is shown above it.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-labels="inside")
					table
						tbody
							tr
								th Spring
								td 58
							tr
								th Summer
								td 72
							tr
								th Autumn
								td 70
							tr
								th Winter
								td 9

			+codeblock('HTML')
				figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-labels="inside")

	+section("Height")
		p The height of a chart is a share of its width. Set it with #[code data-chart-aspect]. The default is #[code 0.38].
		p On a narrow screen, a chart is never less high than #[code --tablechart-min-height]: #[code 200px] by default. Below that width, the height stays the same. Use #[code px], #[code em] or #[code rem], or #[code none] for no minimum.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-aspect="0.6")
				table
					tbody
						tr
							th Spring
							td 58
						tr
							th Summer
							td 72
						tr
							th Autumn
							td 70

	+section("Below zero")
		p A column with a value below zero hangs down from the baseline, with its number under it. The baseline moves up to make room.

		+chart(null, '7-8,10')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6'], ['Feb', '28.7'], ['Mar', '-11.7'], ['Apr', '-36.3'], ['May', '19.8'], ['Jun', '-4.1']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p With several value columns, each column of a group hangs down or stands up on its own.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th 2024
							th 2025
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6', '-2.6'], ['Feb', '28.7', '-12.2'], ['Mar', '-11.7', '12.0'], ['Apr', '-36.3', '26.8'], ['May', '19.8', '-6.9'], ['Jun', '-4.1', '7.5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Grouped columns")
		p If the table has more than one value column, each row is a group, with one column for each value column. The column headers are the names in the legend under the chart.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples sold by variety
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th 2022
							th 2026
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '118', '131', false], ['Granny Johnson', '12', '45', 'granny'], ['Jonagold', '140', '135', false], ['Braeburn', '109', '121', false]]
							tr(class=row[3] || false)
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p Each column of a group is a bit lighter than the one before it. To set a colour for each, use a #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) palette]. A class on a row styles its whole group, as the Granny Johnson does here:

		+style.
			.granny {
				--tablechart-bar-color: #7fb03f;
			}

		p If the numbers are wider than their columns, they all get smaller together, down to 70% of their size. If they still do not fit, none of them are shown. If you point at the row, or tap it, a tooltip shows the numbers of that row.

	+section("Stacked columns")
		p Add #[code data-chart-stack] to the figure. The values of a row are shown on top of each other, the first value column at the bottom. Values below zero are stacked down from the baseline. The total of the row is above the column.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Apples sold
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th Farm shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['2023', '64', '38'], ['2024', '71', '45'], ['2025', '69', '57'], ['2026', '80', '62']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p A number is shown inside its part of the column if it fits. If not, it is left out. If you point at the row, or tap it, a tooltip shows the numbers of that row.
		p Below zero, the parts of a stack go down from the baseline. The total is the sum of all parts, above the column, or under it if the whole stack is below zero.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12', '-4'], ['Feb', '8', '-9'], ['Mar', '-6', '3'], ['Apr', '-14', '-7'], ['May', '4', '-2'], ['Jun', '10', '5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Columns and a line")
		p To show a value column as a line over the columns, add #[code data-chart="line"] to its header. The line uses the same scale as the columns. Each point is in the middle of its column, and arrives with it.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Budget and spend
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Budget
							th(data-chart="line") Spent
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '40', '36'], ['Feb', '42', '45'], ['Mar', '45', '41'], ['Apr', '45', '52'], ['May', '50', '47'], ['Jun', '52', '58']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p Use a line for values that have an order, such as months or years. The legend shows a box for the columns and a line for the line.
		p If a number of the line is on a column, the column is cut away under the number, so the number can be read. The numbers of the columns are inside the columns if they fit. If a point of the line covers a number, or a number has no room, the number is left out. If you point at the row, or tap it, a tooltip shows the numbers of that row.
		p A chart with a line cannot be stacked.

	+section("Many columns")
		-
			const months = ['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June', 'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December'];
			// Two years of visitors to the farm shop.
			const visits = Array.from({ length: 24 }, (_, i) => [
				`${months[i % 12]} ${2024 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				`${months[i % 12].slice(0, 3)} ’${24 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				String(Math.round(60 + i * 1.5 + 25 * Math.cos(((i % 12) - 6) / 6 * Math.PI))),
			]);
		p With many columns, only every second, third or more number is shown, so they do not touch, and the names under the chart get smaller or fewer. Give a row a short name for under the chart with #[code data-short]; the table keeps the full name for a screen reader. See #[a(href=url('/types/line.html#many-rows')) Many rows].

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption: b Visitors to the farm shop
					table
						tbody
							each row in visits
								tr
									th(data-short=row[1])= row[0]
									td= row[2]

			+codeblock('HTML').
				<tr><th data-short="#{visits[0][1]}">#{visits[0][0]}</th><td>#{visits[0][2]}</td></tr>

	+section("A bar chart on narrow screens")
		p A column chart with many rows can be shown as a bar chart when it is narrow. Set #[code --tablechart-type: bar] on the figure, in a container query or a media query. The chart is built again as a bar chart when the query applies, and as a column chart when it does not.

		+style.
			.type-box {
				container-type: inline-size;
			}
			@container (max-width: 500px) {
				.type-box > [data-chart] {
					--tablechart-type: bar;
				}
			}

		p The buttons change the width of the example.

		.width-toggle.lang-toggle(role="group" aria-label="Width of the example")
			button(type="button" data-width="" aria-pressed="true") Wide
			button(type="button" data-width="360px" aria-pressed="false") Narrow
		+chart(null, '1')
			.type-box
				figure(data-chart="column")
					figcaption: b Visitors per month
					table
						tbody
							each row in [['Jan', '41'], ['Feb', '38'], ['Mar', '52'], ['Apr', '61'], ['May', '74'], ['Jun', '88'], ['Jul', '93'], ['Aug', '90'], ['Sep', '77'], ['Oct', '63'], ['Nov', '49'], ['Dec', '45']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]

		script.
			document.querySelectorAll('.width-toggle:not([data-ready])').forEach(function (group) {
				var box = group.nextElementSibling.querySelector('.type-box');
				group.dataset.ready = '';
				group.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(function (button) {
					button.addEventListener('click', function () {
						box.style.maxWidth = button.dataset.width;
						group.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(function (other) {
							other.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(other === button));
						});
					});
				});
			});

		p #[code --tablechart-type] works for column and bar charts. It can also turn a bar chart into a column chart, with #[code --tablechart-type: column].

	+section("Scale break")
		p If one column is much taller than the others, a break in the scale shows part of the scale smaller, so the other columns have room. See #[a(href=url('/features/scale-break.html')) Scale break].

		+chart(null, '10')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '12'], ['Jonagold', '15'], ['Braeburn', '14'], ['Gala', '140']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="axis-break" data-from="20")

	+section("Axis and gridlines")
		p By default there is no value axis: the numbers on the columns show the values. To add numbers along the axis, on the left, add #[code data-chart-axis]. To add a light line at each of those numbers, add #[code data-chart-grid]. You can add one of them, or both. To add them to every chart, set the #[code axis] and #[code grid] options to #[code true].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="column" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b Apples picked
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a chart has an axis or gridlines, its scale ends on a number of the axis. To set the scale yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale], such as #[code data-chart-scale="0 100"]. To change how the axis and gridlines look, see #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Next")
		p #[a(href=url('/features/annotations.html')) Annotations] add a bracket or a trend arrow to a column chart.
